icant heterogeneity (I2 = 85.0%, P< 0.001) (Fig.2a). || There was a significant heterogeneity between studies (I2=78.1%, P = 0.003). || However, significant heterogeneity was found for HDL -C (I2 = 63.2%, P = 0.02). || 95% CI: -1.07, 2.47, P = 0.43) with no significant heterogeneity (I 2 = 0.0%, P =0.97) (Fig.2c). || 95% CI: -1.70, 2.51, P = 0.70) (Fig.2d). || Figure 4b), HDL-C (WMD: -0.04 mg/dL, 95% CI: -2.80, 2.71, P = 0.79; || Figure 4a), LDL-C (WMD: -1.73 mg/dL, 95% CI: - 4.27, 0.80, P = 0.18; || Figure 4c), and TG (WMD: -6.41 mg/dL, 95% CI: -17.26, 4.43, P = 0.16;","body mass index (BMI), waist circumference (WC) , weight, body fat ), fasting blood glucose (FBS) and lipid profiles (triglyceride (TG), total cholesterol (TC), high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C)) in overweight or obese children and adolescents. || The effect of pro-/synbiotics on anthropometric indices (BMI z-score, WC, weight, body fat) The pooled mean difference of 6 studies 20, 22, 25-28 for the effects of pro-/synbiotics on BMI z-score was (WMD: -0.09 z-score; || Pooled analysis did not illustrate any significant changes in BMI z-score, waist circumference, weight, body fat, fasting blood sugar and lipid profiles (triglyceride, total cholesterol, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, and low -density lipoprotein cholesterol ) after supplementation with pro-/synbiotics for 4 -16 weeks. || of trials Effect 1size 95% CI (%) 2I P for heterogeneity BMI z-score Intervention type Probiotic 4 -0.10 -0.34, 0.14 86.3 < 0.001 Synbiotic 2 -0.07 -0.10, -0.04 0.0 72 Duration > 8 weeks 4 -0.12 -0.34, 0.10 81.2 < 0.001 ≤ 8 weeks 2 -0.05 -0.13, 0.03 34.1 0.21 WC Intervention type Probiotic 4 -0.10 -1.95, 2.14 32.4 0.21 Synbiotic 3 -1.12 -3.23, 1.00 3.8 0.35 Duration > 8 weeks 5 0.44 -1.67, 2.56 33.1 0.20 ≤ 8 weeks 2 -1.52 -3.53, 0.48 0.0 0.75 Weight Intervention type Probiotic 2 0.71 -1.12, 2.54 0.0 0.78 Synbiotic || Previous findings revealed that mentioned supplementation was effective on the body weight and WC but not on the BMI, and body fat. || Our analysis of 9 randomized trials did not illustrate any significant changes in BMI z -score, WC, weight, body fat, FBS and lipid profiles after supplementation with probiotics /symbiotic for 4-16 weeks. || ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT 14 Legends of figures: Figure 1: PRISMA flow diagram of study selection process Figure 2: Forest plot of the effect of pro-/synbiotic supplementation on anthropometric indices (BMI z-score, WC, weight, body fat) Figure 3: Forest plot of the effect of pro-/synbiotic supplementation on FBS Figure 4: Forest plot of the effect of pro-/synbiotic supplementation on lipid profiles (LDL-C, HDL- C, TC, TG) ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT 15 Table 1. || First author (publication year) Country Sample size (M/F) Target Population Mean Age RCT design (Blinding) Duration (wks) Intervention of experimental group Intervention of control group Investigated outcomes Vajro (2011) Italy 20 (18/2) Obese children 10 Parallel (yes) 8 CFU 8Lactobacillus rhamnosus Strain GG 12 × 10 Placebo BMI z score Gobel (2012) Denmark 50 (ND) Obese adolescents 13 Parallel (yes) 12 CFU 10L salivarius 10 Placebo BMI z -score, Fat mass, Weight, WC, TG, TC, LDL -C, HDL-C Safavi (2013) Iran 56 (N","body mass index (BMI), waist circumference (WC) , weight, body fat ), fasting blood glucose (FBS) and lipid profiles (triglyceride (TG), total cholesterol (TC), high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C)) in overweight or obese children and adolescents. || No evidence of publication bias was found for TC (P = 0.62, Begg’s ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT 8 test and P = 0.62, Egger’s test), LDL-C (P =1.00, Begg’s test and P =0.38, Egger’s test), HDL-C (P = 0.32, Begg’s test and P = 0.17, Egger’s test), and TG (P =0.85, Begg’s test and P = 0.93, Egger’s test). || Figure 4b), HDL-C (WMD: -0.04 mg/dL, 95% CI: -2.80, 2.71, P = 0.79; || Figure 4a), LDL-C (WMD: -1.73 mg/dL, 95% CI: