PMID:41659802,41659802,Effects of modified fasting therapy on tongue coating and gut microbiome in overweight and obese adults: a controlled clinical trial.,fnut-12-1686416.pdf,1.000,17,17,fnut-12-1686416.pdf,1.000,17,"After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || Predictive analysis of the 30 most significantly altered metabolic pathways (p < 0.05) revealed that the tongue coating and FIGURE 6 Structural shifts in fecal microbiota composition following MFT intervention. || There are also some experiments ( 26) that simultaneously observe the oral cavity and the intestinal tract, but most of them are observation models induced by high-fat diets, lacking active intervention designs. || This dynamic interaction constructs a flexible and efficient host stress nutritional response mechanism and provides a new framework for understanding the systemic impact of dietary interventions via microbiota-driven mechanisms of action (Figure 11). || p = 0.013), Actinomyces (LDA = 4.26; || p = 0.019), Granulicatella (LDA = 3.35; || p = 0.006), and Clostridia (LDA = 3.89;","In the gut microbiota, Bacteroides decreased, and Clostridia increased, with significant upregulation of gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino acid biosynthesis pathways ( p < 0.05). || Post-MFT, tongue microbiota exhibited significantly increased diversity, as indicated by higher Shannon ( p < 0.1) and observed species ( p < 0.05) indices, although the Chao1 index remained unchanged. || Results: In the fasting group, body weight decreased by 4.0 ± 1.6 kg (p < 0.01), BMI decreased by 1.51 ± 0.58 (p < 0.01), significantly, accompanied by marked improvements in blood glucose and lipid profiles (p < 0.05). || *, **, ***, respectively represent the significant differences when p < 0.05, p < 0.01, and p < 0.001. || After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || Comparative distance analyses revealed no statistically significant differences in overall microbial community structure (Supplementary Table 1), suggesting that while diversity increased, the core composition remained largely unchanged. || Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || Predictive analysis of the 30 most significantly altered metabolic pathways (p < 0.05) revealed that the tongue coating and FIGURE 6 Structural shifts in fecal microbiota composition following MFT intervention.","After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || Notably, specific taxa such as Haemophilus and Granulicatella were positively correlated with body weight and BMI (r > 0.4, p < 0.05). || Results: In the fasting group, body weight decreased by 4.0 ± 1.6 kg (p < 0.01), BMI decreased by 1.51 ± 0.58 (p < 0.01), significantly, accompanied by marked improvements in blood glucose and lipid profiles (p < 0.05). || Characteristics/ parameters Fasting group (n = 35) Control group (n = 13) p value Sex (n, %) Male 8,22.8 9,69.2 0.006c Age (years) 40.66 ± 9.81 29.77 ± 6.50 0.073a Height (cm) 162.66 ± 8.21 170.92 ± 9.98 0.496a Weight (kg) Baseline 79.46 ± 16.63 89.77 ± 24.17 0.162a 7 days later 75.46 ± 15.55 89.76 ± 24.11 0.092a Weight change 4.00 ± 1.60 0.00 ± 0.39 <0.001b BMI (kg/m2) Baseline 29.87 ± 4.75 30.39 ± 5.67 0.302a 7 days later 28.36 ± 4.39 30.38 ± 5.65 0.172a BMI change 1.51 ± 0.58 0.00 ± 0.14 <0.001b BMI, body mass i || Characteristics/parameters Baseline post-MFT Change P Weight (kg) 96.4 ± 24.6 91.5 ± 24.3 5.0 ± 1.1 <0.001a BMI (kg/m2) 32.08 ± 5.49 30.41 ± 4.96 1.67 ± 0.57 <0.001a SBP (mmHg) 126.6 ± 16.9 118.6 ± 15.5 8.0 ± 12.7 0.146a DBP (mmHg) 84.9 ± 9.5 75.6 ± 7.7 9.3 ± 7.3 0.015a TC (mmol/L) 4.77 ± 0.42 4.88 ± 0.36 –0.11 ± 0.28 0.315a TG (mmol/L) 1.94 ± 0.68 1.07 ± 0.11 0.88 ± 0.76 0.023a HDL-C (mmol/L) 1.07 ± 0.25 0.97 ± 0.22 0.10 ± 0.09 0.029a LDL-C (mmol/L) 2.85 ± 0.35 3.14 ± 0.34 –0.29 ± 0.34 0.067a FGP (mmol/L) 4.62 ± 0 || Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || It also examines associations between microbial changes and host metabolic indicators such as body weight, body mass index (BMI), blood glucose, and lipid profiles. || This resulted in highly similar baseline BMI values between the MFT group (mean: 29.87 ± 4.75 kg/m2) and the control group (mean: 30.39 ± 5.67 kg/m 2).","Characteristics/parameters Baseline post-MFT Change P Weight (kg) 96.4 ± 24.6 91.5 ± 24.3 5.0 ± 1.1 <0.001a BMI (kg/m2) 32.08 ± 5.49 30.41 ± 4.96 1.67 ± 0.57 <0.001a SBP (mmHg) 126.6 ± 16.9 118.6 ± 15.5 8.0 ± 12.7 0.146a DBP (mmHg) 84.9 ± 9.5 75.6 ± 7.7 9.3 ± 7.3 0.015a TC (mmol/L) 4.77 ± 0.42 4.88 ± 0.36 –0.11 ± 0.28 0.315a TG (mmol/L) 1.94 ± 0.68 1.07 ± 0.11 0.88 ± 0.76 0.023a HDL-C (mmol/L) 1.07 ± 0.25 0.97 ± 0.22 0.10 ± 0.09 0.029a LDL-C (mmol/L) 2.85 ± 0.35 3.14 ± 0.34 –0.29 ± 0.34 0.067a FGP (mmol/L) 4.62 ± 0 || 10.3389/fnut.2025.1686416 Frontiers in Nutrition 03 frontiersin.org weight, BMI, blood lipids, fasting glucose, fasting insulin, and HOMA-IR. || Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || Results: In the fasting group, body weight decreased by 4.0 ± 1.6 kg (p < 0.01), BMI decreased by 1.51 ± 0.58 (p < 0.01), significantly, accompanied by marked improvements in blood glucose and lipid profiles (p < 0.05). || Modified fasting therapy (MFT), a structured form of caloric restriction, has demonstrated improvements in body weight (6), blood glucose (7), insulin sensitivity (8), blood pressure (9), and lipid levels (10). || p = 0.013), Actinomyces (LDA = 4.26; || p = 0.019), Granulicatella (LDA = 3.35;","Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || In the gut microbiota, Bacteroides decreased, and Clostridia increased, with significant upregulation of gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino acid biosynthesis pathways ( p < 0.05). || While prior research has largely focused on the gut—reporting decreases in Firmicutes and increases in Bacteroidetes and Proteobacteria—the tongue microbiome has remained relatively unstudied. || After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || Post-MFT, tongue microbiota exhibited significantly increased diversity, as indicated by higher Shannon ( p < 0.1) and observed species ( p < 0.05) indices, although the Chao1 index remained unchanged. || Predictive analysis of the 30 most significantly altered metabolic pathways (p < 0.05) revealed that the tongue coating and FIGURE 6 Structural shifts in fecal microbiota composition following MFT intervention. || Gut microbial dysbiosis is strongly associated with obesity, while shifts in the oral microbiota— an essential part of the human microbiome—may also affect systemic metabolism (4). || At the phylum level (Figure 3B), the microbiota was dominated by Firmicutes, Bacteroidota, Proteobacteria, Actinobacteriota, and Fusobacteriota, which together accounted for over 90% of total sequences.","Data are reported as mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05. || After the 7-day MFT intervention, the MFT group exhibited significant reductions in body weight and BMI, with mean decreases of 4.00 ± 1.60 kg and 1.51 ± 0.58 kg/m2, respectively (both p < 0.001). || p = 0.013), Actinomyces (LDA = 4.26; || p = 0.019), Granulicatella (LDA = 3.35; || p = 0.006), and Clostridia (LDA = 3.89;","baseline BMI values between the MFT group (mean: 29.87 || mean: 30.39 || mean ± standard deviation, with statistical significance set at p < 0.05 || Baseline characteristics for both groups are summarized in Table 1 || mean decreases of 4.00 || Baseline 79.46 || change 4.00 || Baseline 29.87 || change 1.51 || reduction analyses, including principal component analysis (PCA) ( Figure 4 || Baseline post-MFT Change P Weight (kg) 96.4 || week fasting therapy in patients with Type-2","Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effects of modified fasting therapy on tongue coating and gut microbiome in overweight and obese adults: a controlled clinical trial Dongkai Zeng †, Tingying Zhang †, Yunling Zhu , Jiahao Feng , Ziheng Ye , Jin Zhao , Peng Huang , Li Zhang * and Taoli Liu * Traditional Chinese Medicine Department, The Seventh Affiliated Hospital of Sun Yat-sen University, Shenzhen, China Intr",control || intervention || diet || probiotic,116 g || 20 g,3 days || 2 days || 7 days,48 participants || n = 35 || n = 13 || 48 patients || 17 patients || n = 7,ok,
PMID:41479657,41479657,"Targeting gut microbiota with short-chain fructo-oligosaccharides prebiotic fibers to support metabolic health in overweight prediabetic adults: a randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled study.",fnut-12-1718169.pdf,1.000,14,14,fnut-12-1718169.pdf,1.000,14,"However, findings remain conflicting, probably due to variability in the study populations’ health status, diet and lifestyle habits, type and dose of prebiotic, and intervention duration ( 15, 16). || Clinical trials with other prebiotic fibers such as inulin and oligofructose have yielded inconsistent results, with outcomes varying depending on the population studied, dietary context, type and dose of prebiotic, and intervention duration (15, 16). || 10.3389/fnut.2025.1718169 Frontiers in Nutrition 03 frontiersin.org suspected food allergy or intolerance or hypersensitivity to any food ingredient, taking or having stopped less than 3 months before V1 any treatment which could significantly affect assessment criteria according to the investigator (including dietary supplements, pro- and prebiotics, and antibiotics), with a significant change in food habits or in physical activity in the past 3 months, with a specific diet (hyper or hypocaloric, vegan, vegetarian || In addition to a significant increase in Bifidobacterium ( p = 0.0202), scFOS supplementation enriched Anaerostipes, while reducing Blautia and Ruminococcus2 ( p < 0.05). || Regarding total SCFAs, the level tended to increase with scFOS supplementation and to decrease with placebo (mean change: +24.6 vs -32.2 μmol/g in the ITT population), although the treatment effect did not reach statistical significance ( p = 0.1234 in the ITT population; || During the intervention period, participants were instructed to consume orally two 10 g sachets of prebiotic per day (Actilight®; || Prebiotic dietary fibre intervention improves fecal markers related to inflammation in obese patients: results from the Food4gut randomized placebo-controlled trial. || The increase in acetate and propionate contrasts with some studies in obese individuals supplemented with oligofructose-inulin (37, 58) but aligns with results from a recent study in individuals with T2D consuming prebiotics (30).","In the ITT population, fecal acetate and propionate levels increased in the scFOS arm between V2 and V5 (median change: +12.1 μmol/g and +3.1 μmol/g respectively) whereas they decreased in the placebo arm (median change: -12.5 μmol/g and -5.1 μmol/g respectively), leading to a significant difference between arms ( p = 0.031 and p = 0.0062 respectively). || 3.2 Glucose metabolism No significant difference was observed between scFOS and placebo in the change of HbA1c from V2 to V5 (+0.055% vs +0.030%, p = 0.6835) in the ITT population. || *p ≤ 0.05: statistically significant difference between arms, as per the Wilcoxon-rank sum test (acetate: p = 0.0310; || Conversely, the change in butyrate level did not differ significantly between treatment arms ( p = 0.1249) ( Figure 4). || However, the difference in the change from V2 to V5 of taxonomic β-diversity was close to significance level when analyzed through Bray-Curtis (p = 0.0758) and Jaccard (p = 0.0663), the increase of taxonomic β-diversity tending to be higher in the scFOS arm compared to the placebo arm (Figure 3B). || 3.4 Gut microbiota: composition and fermentative activity Regarding gut microbiota analyzed in a subpopulation of 30 participants, absolute changes of 3 α-diversity indices out of the 4 were statistically significant: Observed Richness, Inverse Simpson and Shannon indices decreased between V2 and V5 in the scFOS arm (p < 0.004; || Bold values indicate statistically significant differences between arms (p < 0.05). || These changes were accompanied by increased fecal acetate ( p = 0.0310) and propionate ( p = 0.0062), contrasting with decreases in placebo.","p < 0.1): body weight and BMI tended to increase in the placebo arm, while remaining relatively stable in the scFOS arm (Table 3). || One study reported a reduction in body weight following 30 g/day of inulin for 6 weeks (34), and a decrease in fat mass was observed when inulin supplementation was extended to 18 weeks and combined with a weight loss program (35). || In obese individuals, inulin supplementation at 16 g/day for 12 weeks did not significantly affect anthropometric or metabolic TABLE 3 Absolute change from V2 of body weight and body mass index per treatment arm in the ITT population. || *p ≤ 0.05: statistically significant difference between arms, as per the Wilcoxon-rank sum test (acetate: p = 0.0310; || Conversely, the change in butyrate level did not differ significantly between treatment arms ( p = 0.1249) ( Figure 4). || However, body composition outcomes favored scFOS: fat mass decreased (medians: -0.26% vs +0.20%, p = 0.0273), lean mass increased (+0.27% vs -0.30%; || In addition to a significant increase in Bifidobacterium ( p = 0.0202), scFOS supplementation enriched Anaerostipes, while reducing Blautia and Ruminococcus2 ( p < 0.05). || 3.2 Glucose metabolism No significant difference was observed between scFOS and placebo in the change of HbA1c from V2 to V5 (+0.055% vs +0.030%, p = 0.6835) in the ITT population.","3.2 Glucose metabolism No significant difference was observed between scFOS and placebo in the change of HbA1c from V2 to V5 (+0.055% vs +0.030%, p = 0.6835) in the ITT population. || No significant treatment effect was observed for glucose metabolism markers, including the primary outcome HbA1c (+0.055% with scFOS vs +0.030% with placebo, p = 0.6835). || The Blautia genus has been found in higher abundance in individuals with prediabetes ( 2) and positively correlated with glycemic and anthropometric markers such as fasting glucose, HOMA-IR, HbA1c and waist circumference (2, 38). || Participants were excluded if biological analyses performed on the blood sample at V1 was out of range: fasting blood triglycerides (TG) > 3.5 