PMID:41830022,41830022,Green Tea Catechin Plus Inulin Improves Insulin Resistance Without Reducing Visceral Fat and Shows Exploratory Gut Microbiota Signals in Adults with Visceral Obesity: A Double-Blind Randomized Controlled Trial.,nutrients-18-00851.pdf,1.000,15,15,nutrients-18-00851.pdf,1.000,15,"The between-group difference in VFA change was not statistically significant (p = 0.243), indicating that the intervention did not produce a clear reduction in VFA compared with placebo under the present conditions (Figure 2). || Previous studies have reported that lower abun- dances of butyrate-producing bacteria and reduced SCFA levels are associated with obesity, insulin resistance, and metabolic syndrome [10,11], whereas dietary fiber interventions that increase SCFA production can ameliorate these conditions [8–10]. || Reduced SCFA concentrations and depletion of butyrate-producing bacte- ria have been observed in obesity and type 2 diabetes [10,11], whereas dietary interventions with fermentable fibers that increase SCFA production are often associated with improve- ments in insulin resistance and inflammatory markers [ 8–10]. || Second, while the catechin dose in this study (400 mg/day) falls within the lower range of doses used in earlier trials, several positive studies employed higher doses of catechins (e.g., 500–600 mg/day or more) and sometimes combined catechin intake with additional lifestyle interventions such as caloric restriction or exercise [3–5]. || +0.18 ± 0.76), with a statistically significant between-group difference (p = 0.020). || In contrast, the change in Bifidobacterium relative abundance was not clearly associated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = 0, p = 0.95). || CAP , an index of liver fat, changed by-2.0 ± 58.5 dB/m (n = 44) in thecatechin + inulin group and by -6.8 ± 64.1 dB/m (n = 42) in the placebo group over 12 weeks, with no significant between-group difference (p = 0.72). || The change in Coprococcus relative abundance from baseline to week 12 was inversely correlated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = -0.28, p = 0.010), indicating that participants with greater increases in Coprococcus tended to exhibit greater improvements in HOMA-IR (Figure 3).","HOMA-IR decreased in the catechin + inulin group and increased in the placebo group, yielding a significant between-group difference (mean change: -0.32 vs. || CAP , an index of liver fat, changed by-2.0 ± 58.5 dB/m (n = 44) in thecatechin + inulin group and by -6.8 ± 64.1 dB/m (n = 42) in the placebo group over 12 weeks, with no significant between-group difference (p = 0.72). || The between-group difference in VFA change was not statistically significant (p = 0.243), indicating that the intervention did not produce a clear reduction in VFA compared with placebo under the present conditions (Figure 2). || +0.18 ± 0.76), with a statistically significant between-group difference (p = 0.020). || From baseline to week 12, HOMA-IR decreased in the catechin + inulin group and increased in the placebo group (mean change: -0.32 ± 1.71 vs. || In contrast, the change in Bifidobacterium relative abundance was not clearly associated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = 0, p = 0.95). || AST and ALT increased modestly in the catechin + inulin group, whereas they remained relatively stable in the placebo group, resulting in statistically significant between-group differences. || The change in Coprococcus relative abundance from baseline to week 12 was inversely correlated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = -0.28, p = 0.010), indicating that participants with greater increases in Coprococcus tended to exhibit greater improvements in HOMA-IR (Figure 3).","Supplementation with inulin or inulin-type fructans (ITFs) has been reported to selectively increase beneficial bacteria such as Bifidobac- terium spp., enhance the production of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), and thereby reduce body weight, BMI, body fat mass, and waist circumference, as well as improve fasting glucose, 