) compared to the HGI diet (␀ 1.03 ± 5.09%), although this difference w as not statistically significant (p = 0.4, r = -0.26). || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Acetic acid significantly decreased after HGI (p = 0.028) and show ed a non- significant increase after LGI (p = 0.31), also yielding a large ef- fect (r = 0.70).","A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || Additionally, studies indicate that liver fat reduction can occur without significant changes in body weight or BMI in pa- tients with MASLD or metabolic syndrome [45–47]. || Fractional change from baseline show ed a significant mean 